            <ArticleTitle>Short-term gain for long-term pain? Which patients should be treated and should we ration?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>148-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0268355513476815</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Treatments of common conditions which do not affect mortality often become sidelined in the drive to improve efficiency and reduce costs. The rationing of patients is a divisive but crucial component to universal health care. How should this be accomplished?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In this article we examine the outcomes of various rationing methods in varicose veins.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">No method is perfect and treatment of symptoms and complications should remain the target for all physicians.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
